An AI notetaker joins your calls, records them, and sends back a transcript, a summary and a list of action items. Three names come up most often: Otter, Fireflies and Fathom. All three have a free plan, so the useful question is where each free plan stops and what the paid plans cost once it does. This guide compares the published prices and limits as of 12 November 2026, in US dollars per user per month. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the vendor pages. Stackbrook did not test these tools, and has no affiliate relationship with any of them at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.

One note on sources. Fathom's figures come from its pricing page, Fireflies' from its help guide and Otter's from its pricing page. Each vendor's own blog posts sometimes give different limits from its pricing pages, and where they conflict this guide says so.

## The prices at a glance

| Plan | Otter | Fireflies | Fathom |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Free | $0 | $0 | $0 |
| Entry paid, annual billing | Pro, $8.33 | Pro, $10 | Premium, $16 |
| Entry paid, monthly billing | Pro, $16.99 | Pro, $18 | Premium, $20 |
| Team or business, annual billing | Business, $19.99 | Business, $19 | Team, $15, and Business, $25 (2-user minimum) |
| Team or business, monthly billing | Business, $30 | Business, $29 | Team, $19, and Business, $34 |
| Enterprise | Custom | $39, annual only | Listed, with SSO and SCIM |

All three charge per user, and billing monthly instead of annually costs noticeably more at every paid tier, so decide which you can commit to before comparing.

![Bar chart of annual-billing prices per user per month: entry paid plans Otter Pro $8.33, Fireflies Pro $10, Fathom Premium $16; business plans Otter $19.99, Fireflies $19, Fathom $25](/images/site1/ai-meeting-notetakers-pricing-compared-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the vendors' published prices, as cited in this article.*

## The free plans

This is where the three differ most.

**Fathom.** Its pricing page lists unlimited recordings and transcriptions, instant AI call summaries, clips, playlists and search across calls, with a choice of bot capture or a bot-free option that the page marks as beta and Mac-only. It lists no minute cap.

**Otter.** The free Basic plan gives 300 transcription minutes a month and a 30-minute limit per conversation. Otter's pricing page lists 3 file imports for the lifetime of the account, not per month, and Otter's blog adds 20 AI Chat queries. Because the minutes are a monthly pool, a few long meetings can use them up.

**Fireflies.** Its guide says Free is $0 and that its Talk dictation feature is included on every plan. Fireflies' blog posts describe unlimited transcription on Free, though its help articles tie free transcription to the Auto Join feature and describe a small number of free transcriptions without it, so read the live plan details. The sources also disagree on the other limits: a recent comparison page says 3 AI summaries a month and 800 minutes of storage per seat pooled across the team, while an older pricing post says 400 minutes of storage per team and unlimited summaries. Its billing guide lists 20 one-time AI credits on Free. Treat the numbers as unsettled and check the live plan before relying on any of them.

## What the paid plans add

**Fathom** keeps the price steps simple. Premium adds advanced call summaries, AI-generated action items, a conversational meeting assistant and a custom meeting bot. Team, with a two-user minimum, adds a shared workspace with folders, comments and playlists, trackers, and Ask Fathom across team calls. Business adds CRM field sync, a deal view and coaching scorecards. Every paid plan carries a 90-day money-back guarantee, though the page does not set out the terms.

**Fireflies** says Pro adds video recording, file downloads, AI Skills and unlimited integrations, and Business adds unlimited storage, a multilingual mode and team analytics. CRM autofill and deal tracking are Business and Enterprise features. Enterprise adds items such as SSO and a HIPAA agreement.

**Otter** raises the limits at each step. Pro is 1,200 monthly minutes, and its pricing page lists 90 minutes per conversation and 10 file imports a month, though Otter's blog posts say up to 4-hour conversations. On Business, the pricing page lists unlimited monthly meeting transcription and 6,000 minutes for imported files, while some Otter blog posts describe the 6,000 as the monthly transcription allowance, so confirm the live page. CRM sync on Business covers up to 5 users per Otter's blog. Enterprise is custom, with SSO and HIPAA options. Otter's posts quote Business at $19.99 on annual billing in most places and at $20 in a couple, so expect a rounding difference at checkout.

## Which fits which situation

- **You want free and unlimited.** Fathom's free plan is the only one of the three listed without a minute or summary cap on its page. Fireflies is close on transcription but check its storage limit, and Otter's 300 minutes is the tightest.
- **You want the lowest paid price for yourself.** Otter Pro at $8.33 on annual billing is lowest, but it comes with a 1,200-minute monthly limit and per-conversation caps. Fireflies Pro is $10, so compare its storage and recording rules for your meetings.
- **You run a small team.** Fathom's Team plan starts at $15 per user with two users, and the other two start with their business plans at about $19 to $20. Compare what each plan includes, not just the price.
- **You sell and need CRM sync.** The CRM features sit on Fathom Business and Fireflies Business, and Otter's Business and Enterprise plans. Check that your CRM is on the vendor's integration list.

## Before you pay

Run your own call through the free plans and look at the transcript, the summary and what you can export. Check whether participants see a bot join, and whether you have consent to record, since recording rules depend on where the participants are. Ownership changes can also affect terms: Superhuman announced on 14 September 2026 that it had acquired Fathom. Fathom's pricing page did not mention it when we checked, so look for any change to its terms or plans before you commit to a long contract.
